'' 'procptr( T.+= )' NAMES A MEMBER BY ITS OPERATOR TOKEN, and the member parser
'' refused one: "expected a member name after '.'". The name is the token table's TEXT
'' column, exactly as the DECLARATION takes it, so the two agree without either
'' knowing about the other -- and '[]' is two tokens and one name in both places.
'' Only inside a procptr argument, because an operator after a dot is a syntax error
'' anywhere else and should stay one.
''
'' pointers/procptr-member.bas writes it twelve times.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/procptr-operator.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr _ZN1TpLEu7INTEGER
'' EXPECT-OUT: func.addr ptr _ZN1TixEu7INTEGER
'' GOLDEN: expected/procptr-operator.out
